The catalogue has two levels. A **title** is the book as published: name, authors, ISBN, subjects,
price. A **copy** is one physical item with its own barcode, condition and shelf. Lending always
involves a copy.

**You need:** **Search the catalogue** to look, **Add & edit books and copies** to change, **Bulk
import** for the spreadsheet.

## Search

Open **Library → Catalogue**. Search matches the beginnings of words in titles, authors and similar
fields. **Include hidden books** shows titles the school keeps out of the parent portal. Open a book
to see its copies and who holds them, and a copy to see its history.

## Add a book

1. Select **Add book**.
2. Fill in the title and details. If **ISBN lookup** is on in Library Settings, enter the ISBN and
   select **Look up** to fill empty fields from Open Library.
3. Choose where the book is visible: everyone, or staff only.
4. Add copies. You can add up to 200 at once. Choose the condition, the shelf location and whether it
   was bought or donated.

Copies without a barcode of their own get a generated label such as `LIB000123`.

## Import a book register

1. In the Catalogue select **Import**, then **Download a template** if you want one.
2. **Choose .xlsx file** and map your column headings, starting with **Title**.
3. Review the plan: **New books**, **Copies of books already in the catalogue**, **Copies keeping
   their barcode**, **Copies with new labels**, **Rows skipped**, and any rows to look at with the
   reason for each.
4. Confirm. Nothing is held between the plan and the commit, so the plan is recalculated against the
   catalogue as it is at that moment.

Good to know:
- Rows with the same ISBN become one title. Without an ISBN, a title is matched by name and first
  author.
- A row **with** a barcode is one copy with that label. Importing the same file again adds nothing
  twice.
- A row **without** a barcode gets new labels every time it is imported, so a re-import adds
  duplicates. The plan warns you.
- One file can hold up to 20,000 rows.

## Print barcode labels

Select **Print labels**, tick the copies, and choose which slot on the first sheet to start from
(partly used label sheets). The layout is A4, 3 columns by 8. Newly generated labels are ticked by
default, because those are the ones with nothing on the book yet.

## Scan with the camera

Anywhere the app asks for a scan you can use **Scan with camera**. It takes the same path as a
handheld scanner. It closes after one code, except in a stock-take where it stays open.

## Withdraw, repair, restore

A librarian with **Withdraw, repair & restore copies** can mark a copy as repair, withdrawn or
available. Missing and lost are set by stock-takes and the desk.

